course: dictionary
lesson: 12772
title: Economise: spending less and saving
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Economise is a verb. It means to spend less money, or to use less of something, so that you save it. Look for the clues around it: the words about money, saving and cutting back tell you what it means.
explain: «Our family has little money, so we need to economise this month.» The sentence says money is short, so economise must mean spend less. Test it: "we need to spend less this month" still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: You can economise on one thing. To economise on something is to cut back on that one thing. «We economised on water by taking shorter showers.» The family cut back on water, so they used less and saved.
explain: Economising is not the same as refusing to spend or being mean. It means being careful and choosing where to save, so the money lasts. Many stories show a poor family who economises by eating simple meals and mending old clothes.
fact: Economise is a verb: economise, economises, economised, economising.
fact: «Economise» means to spend less money, or use less of something, so that you save.
fact: To economise on something means to cut back on that one thing.
fact: In American English the spelling is «economize».
fact: Families, schools and councils often economise by saving energy and water, such as turning off lights in empty rooms.
exercise: «Our family has little money, so we need to economise this month.» What does economise mean? | Give money away to friends | Spend more money on treats | *Spend less money so that you save | Lose money in the shops
exercise: «We economised on water by taking shorter showers.» What did the family cut back on? | Breakfast | *Water | Holidays
exercise: Dad says, «Let's economise on snacks, not on school trips.» What does economise on snacks mean? | *Cut back on snacks so you spend less | Buy more snacks for everyone | Eat snacks in bed | Share snacks with the class
test: The school decided to economise by turning off lights in empty rooms. What does economise mean here? | To close the school | To spend more on lights | To paint the walls | *To use less so that you save
test: «Ruby had to economise on new clothes, so she mended her old coat.» What did Ruby cut back on? | *New clothes | Food | Her coat | Her school bag
exam: «The family could not afford a holiday, so they economised on everything they could.» What does economised mean in this sentence? | Gave their money to charity | Lost their money on a trip | Spent more money on fun | *Cut back to save money
